    <figcaption>Digital tools should be accessible — not locked away behind paywalls.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Anyone looking for useful digital tools today almost inevitably ends up in a subscription trap: a simple text generator, an image optimizer, or a smart everyday assistant demands 19 or 29 euros a month after just three clicks. Artificial intelligence was supposed to make work easier and democratize opportunity — instead, it has become a market of countless overpriced wrapper apps that lock basic functionality behind rigid paywalls.
  </p>

  <p>
    From Uster, in the canton of Zurich, KodiniTools offers a counter-model: smart software that stays directly accessible, with no hidden costs and no subscription requirement.
  </p>

  <h2>The Philosophy: Software as a Tool, Not a Hostage</h2>

  <p>
    Behind the project is <a href="https://kodinitools.com/uebermich" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dinko Ramic</a>, a developer from Uster, Switzerland. The core idea of KodiniTools is simple: useful technology belongs in the hands of the people who need it — not just those willing to fund dozens of monthly subscriptions.
  </p>

  <p>Many commercial platforms engineer artificial scarcity:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Limited tokens</strong> for standard tasks</li>
    <li><strong>Forced upgrades</strong> after just a few inputs</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-renewing subscription models</strong> that bet on users forgetting to cancel</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    KodiniTools breaks with this pattern. The tools are designed from the ground up to be fast, functional, and usable without restrictions — directly in the browser, with no registration hurdles or bait-and-switch offers.
  </p>

  <h2>Voluntary Donations Instead of Paywalls: The Community Agreement</h2>

  <p>The obvious question is often: "If everything is free — what's the catch?"</p>

  <p>There isn't one. KodiniTools is built on a model of mutual respect:</p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Full Access</strong>
        <p>Every tool is available to every user free of charge and fully functional.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Voluntary Appreciation</strong>
        <p>Anyone who uses the tools regularly, saves time, or wants to support the idea behind the project can make a voluntary donation.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Sustainable Development</strong>
        <p>Server costs, ongoing development, and new features are funded by people who have already experienced the value — not extracted upfront, before anyone even knows whether the software helps.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    This model shifts the focus: away from chasing monthly recurring revenue (MRR), toward honest software quality that convinces people on its own merit.
  </p>

  <h2>Swiss Pragmatism on the Web</h2>

  <p>
    Instead of inflated marketing promises, developer Dinko Ramic relies on Swiss precision and pragmatism: lean interfaces, fast load times, and concrete solutions for specific problems.
  </p>

  <p>
    KodiniTools proves that even in the age of commercial AI hype, there is still room for independent software development — driven by passion, purpose, and trust in a fair community.
  </p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Transparency &amp; Fairness as the Standard:</strong> Those who build software that provides real value don't need to lock users in. Those who choose to support it voluntarily do so out of conviction — keeping the web a little more open for everyone.</p>
  </div>

